                %a{:href => "#see_also"}  See also
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A membership relation is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relation"} binary relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            found in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+type+theory"} simply sorted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            between terms which can be elements and terms which can be sets. Membership relations are found both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
          %p
            This is in contrast to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependently sorted
            set theories, where membership
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            is a typing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            rather than a binary relation.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p Membership relations have different properties in different set theories which can be distinguished.
          %h3#homogeneous_and_heterogeneous_membership_relations Homogeneous and heterogeneous membership relations
          %p
            Recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relation"} binary relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            takes values in sorts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is
            %strong homogeneous
            or an
            %strong endorelation
            if it takes values in the same sort
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %strong heterogeneous
            if it takes values in two distinct sorts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            The membership relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            is homogeneous if both sets and elements are terms of the same sort, while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            is heterogeneous if sets and elements are terms of different sorts. For example, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFA"} ZFA
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"} fully formal ETCS
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            are homogeneous, while in the traditional presentation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            as a two-sorted theory, the membership relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            is heterogeneous.
          %h3#material_and_structural_membership_relations Material and structural membership relations
          %p
            Another important difference is the distinction between the membership relations in material and structural set theories, in what we could call
            %strong material membership relation
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %strong structural membership relation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            in set theories with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homogeneous+membership+relations"} homogeneous membership relations
          %p
            A set theory with a homogeneous membership relation is a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            if it has a material membership relations, and it is a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            if it has a structural membership relation. If the set theory has multiple homogeneous membership relations, the set theory is a
            %strong structural set theory
            if there exists a structural membership relation, and the set theory is a
            %strong material set theory
            if every homogeneous membership relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+membership+relation"} material membership relation
          %p A few definitions have been proposed to distinguish between homogeneous membership relations in material and structural set theories:
          %h4#structural_membership_relations_without_quine_atoms Structural membership relations without Quine atoms
          %p
            Both material set theories like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFA"} ZFA
            and structural set theories like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"} fully formal ETCS
            have a homogeneous membership relation on the theory (and fully formal ETCS has multiple homogeneous membership relations). This means that merely having a homogeneous membership relation is not sufficient for the membership relation to be a material membership relation.
          %p One proposed difference between material set theory and structural set theory is that in material set theory, elements have internal structure and/or sets are the internal structure of some other objects, while in structural set theory, elements do not have internal structure and sets are not the internal structure of other objects.
          %p
            Thus, an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            %strong has internal structure
            if there exists an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in e
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %strong is an internal structure
            if there exists an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \in a
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} hasInternalStructure
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isElement
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi a
                  %mo .
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{hasInternalStructure}(e) \coloneqq \mathrm{isElement}(e) \wedge \exists a.a \in e
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isInternalStructure
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi a
                  %mo .
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{isInternalStructure}(S) \coloneqq \mathrm{isSet}(S) \wedge \exists a.S \in a
          %p
            If an set theory only has one homogeneous membership relation, then the membership relation is a
            %strong material membership relation
            if there exists an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            which has internal structure or a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            which is an internal structure, and the membership relation is a
            %strong structural membership relation
            if for all elements
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            does not have internal structure, and for all sets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is not an internal structure.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isMaterial
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi a
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} hasInternalStructure
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isInternalStructure
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{isMaterial} \coloneqq \exists a.(\mathrm{hasInternalStructure}(a) \vee \mathrm{isInternalStructure}(a))
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isStructural
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi a
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} hasInternalStructure
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isInternalStructure
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{isStructural} \coloneqq \forall a.(\neg\mathrm{hasInternalStructure}(a) \wedge \neg\mathrm{isInternalStructure}(a))
          %p
            Thus, the membership relation in a theory like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            is a material membership relation because sets and elements are the same, and thus all elements have internal structure and all sets are internal structure. The same is true of any theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+sets"} pure sets
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/New+Foundations"} New Foundations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/urelements"} urelements
            such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFA"} ZFA
          %p
            In contrast, the homogeneous membership relation in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"} fully formal ETCS
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in b \coloneqq s(a) = 1 \wedge s(b) = 0 \wedge t(a) = t(b)
          %p
            where the constant primitive
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is the identity morphism of the terminal object and the constant primitive
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is the identity morphism of the initial object, and where sets and elements are defined as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{isSet}(a) \coloneqq s(b) = 0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isElement
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{isElement}(a) \coloneqq s(b) = 1
          %p is a structural membership relation, because it can be proven that every element does not have internal structure and every set is not internal structure.
          %h4#structural_membership_relations_with_quine_atoms Structural membership relations with Quine atoms
          %p
            The above definition states that in a structural set theory with a homogeneous membership relation,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quine+atoms"} Quine atoms
            cannot exist. However, this means that in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"} fully formal ETCS
            when sets are defined as identity functions or as functions with singleton codomain, the defined membership relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            is a material membership relation, as in both cases the singleton is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quine+atom"} Quine atom
            with respect to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
          %p
            However, since the subset of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+functions"} identity functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            are all in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            with each other, it should not matter which subset of function to designate as the sets. This motivates an alternate definition of structural and material membership relations, which allow for Quine atoms to exist with respect to a structural membership relation.
          %p
            A membership relation is a
            %strong structural membership relation
            if each connected component of the respective membership graph is either an edgeless vertex, a rooted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree"} tree
            whose children are all leaves, or possibly a vertex with a single loop:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the edgeless vertex represents the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            %li
              %p
                the root of the rooted tree represents a set with more than one element, as well as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
                in presentations of unsorted structural set theories where the singleton is different from the element in the singleton. The children leaves of the root represent the elements of the set.
            %li
              %p
                the vertex with a single loop represents a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quine+atom"} Quine atom
                with respect to the membership relation
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∈
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+functions"} identity functions
                or as functions with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
                is always a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quine+atom"} Quine atom
          %p
            A
            %strong material membership relation
            is then a membership relation whose membership graphs have more complex structure than those of structural membership relations.
          %h2#see_also  See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
